
==========================================================================
A. Lunar heat-flow experiment: derived quantities
==========================================================================
VERIFIED INPUTS (V)
  measured heat flow            q    = 17 mW/m^2
  measured thermal gradient     dT/dz = 1.5-2.0 K/m
  regolith density              1.1-2.1 g/cc
  radiative share at noon (top few cm) = 70%
  heater settings               0.002-0.5 W
  gradient thermometers at      28 and 47 cm

DERIVED: regolith thermal conductivity from Fourier's law, k = q / (dT/dz)
  gradient 1.5 K/m  ->  k =  11.33 mW/(m.K) = 1.13e-02 W/(m.K)
  gradient 2.0 K/m  ->  k =   8.50 mW/(m.K) = 8.50e-03 W/(m.K)
  => k = 8.5-11.3 mW/(m.K), central 9.7 mW/(m.K)
  DERIVED HERE, NOT RETRIEVED.  For scale: intact igneous rock conducts
  at ~2-4 W/(m.K), so loose regolith at ~9.7 mW/(m.K) is roughly 300x
  LESS conductive -- which is exactly what a granular powder with point
  contacts should be, and radiative transfer across those contacts is the
  standard reason for it.  HONEST QUALIFICATION: the MEASURED 70% radiative
  share applies only to the top few cm at noon, whereas the derived k is a
  bulk value over the whole probe depth, so the radiative share cannot be
  named as 'the mechanism' for k itself -- only as an example of the class
  of effect.  (The ~2-4 W/(m.K) rock figure is a conventional textbook
  value, NOT verified in this session; it is used only as an
  order-of-magnitude comparison.)

DERIVED, AND IT CORRECTS AN IMPLICATION: how far can the surface
  gradient be extrapolated downward?
  linear extrapolation to 300 km: 1.5 K/m x 3.00e5 m = 4.500e+05 K
  linear extrapolation to 300 km: 2.0 K/m x 3.00e5 m = 6.000e+05 K
  The measured surface gradient sustained to 300 km would give ~5e5 K --
  ~300x above the ~1.5e3 K at which mantle silicates melt, i.e. about THREE
  orders of magnitude, not four.  So the surface measurement ALONE does not
  give the deep temperature, and the source's statement
  that 'temperatures [at] ~300 km' would be 'relatively close to melting'
  is a MODELLED result, not an extrapolation of the gradient that HFE
  measured.  This is recorded because it is the one place where the
  source's framing could be over-read: what HFE measured is the shallow
  gradient and the heat flux, and the deep structure comes from elsewhere.

DERIVED: total lunar heat output, q x (4 pi R_Moon^2)
  lunar surface area           = 3.7932e+13 m^2 (37.932 x 10^12 m^2)
  total heat output at 17 mW/m^2 = 6.448e+11 W = 645 GW
  For scale only, and NOT asserted from a source retrieved this session:
  Earth's total surface heat flow is commonly quoted near 4.7 x 10^13 W
  (47 TW).  The Moon's output is then about 1 part in 73 of Earth's -- and the Moon's mass is
  about 1 part in 81, so the two bodies' heat output PER UNIT MASS comes
  out within ~10% of each other:
    Moon  6.448e+11 W / 7.346e+22 kg = 8.78e-12 W/kg
    Earth 4.700e+13 W / 5.972e+24 kg = 7.87e-12 W/kg  (Earth figure flagged NOT verified)
  This matters because it is a scale check on the HFE number: a value
  that were wildly wrong would not land within a factor of ~1 of Earth's
  per-unit-mass figure.  It is NOT evidence about Apollo on its own.

SENSITIVITY: heat flow over a plausible band
  q = 12.0 mW/m^2 -> k =  6.00- 8.00 mW/(m.K)  -> global output  455 GW
  q = 17.0 mW/m^2 -> k =  8.50-11.33 mW/(m.K)  -> global output  645 GW
  q = 22.0 mW/m^2 -> k = 11.00-14.67 mW/(m.K)  -> global output  835 GW
  q = 30.0 mW/m^2 -> k = 15.00-20.00 mW/(m.K)  -> global output 1138 GW

