B. Apollo lunar seismic network: durability and what it took to put it there
==========================================================================
VERIFIED INPUTS (V)
  Wikipedia, 'Moonquake': 'instruments placed by the Apollo 12, 14, 15 and
   16 missions functioned perfectly until they were switched off in 1977';
   'Between 1972 and 1977, 28 shallow moonquakes were observed'; shallow
   events up to mB = 5.5; deep events ~700 km down, probably tidal.
  Wikipedia, 'Apollo Lunar Surface Experiments Package': Apollo 11's
   EASEP seismometer 'was sensitive enough to detect Neil Armstrong's
   movements during sleep.'
  Wikipedia, 'Apollo 12': the LM ascent stage was fired into the Moon as a
   ~1 ton of TNT calibration event.

  Apollo 12 landed 19 Nov 1969 (day-of-year 323); ALSEP shut off 30 Sep 1977
  => continuous unattended seismic coverage = 7.86 years (2870 days)
     (Apollo 12's OWN instrument ran the whole interval; Apollo 14/15/16
     were added from 1971-72, so the NETWORK's span is 5.77 yr, 1972-1977,
     matching the window over which the 28 shallow events were logged.)
     network-era span = 5.75 yr

  shallow moonquakes: 28 events logged
     over the 5.0 yr 1972-1977 window used here -> 5.6 /yr by the network
     over 6 calendar years (1972..1977 inclusive)   -> 4.7 /yr
     The divisor is an ASSUMPTION (A): the exact span of the window is
     not stated by the source, which says only 'Between 1972 and 1977'.
     Both values are given so the reader can choose.  All four stations
     heard each event, so this is a network detection rate, not per station.

  Scales of sensitivity, as arithmetic:
   * the Apollo 11 seismometer detected a sleeping astronaut MOVING INSIDE
     the LM -- a ~10 kg mass displaced a few metres away, on an instrument
     in vacuum with no atmospheric or oceanic noise.
   * the Apollo 12 LM ascent stage was deliberately crashed as a ~1 ton of
     TNT equivalent calibration event -- a signal from a KNOWN, known-mass
     source at a known time and place, used to calibrate lunar travel-time
     curves.  Its mass is not asserted here (not verified this session).
   * lunar shaking from a shallow event 'can last for up to an hour',
     because there is little damping -- a direct, dated consequence of
     the Moon having no oceans and no significant atmosphere.

  What an Apollo staging hoax would have had to accomplish.  Under the
  staging hypothesis, no Apollo hardware went to the Moon, so everything the
  program LEFT there must have been delivered robotically and have gone on
  reporting, unprompted, for years.  Scoped to Apollo only (the Lunokhod and
  Chandrayaan-3 arrays are INDEPENDENT hardware and are not the hoax's
  problem -- the correct statement is that a faked Apollo would have had to
  COEXIST with them, not to place them):
    3 retroreflectors (Apollo 11/14/15) returning pulses to this day;
    5 autonomous radioisotope-powered ALSEP stations + EASEP, operating
      to 30 Sep 1977 and still received by RATAN-600 in Oct-Nov 1977;
    4 seismometers that detected moonquakes nobody had predicted;
    2 heat-flow stations that produced a self-consistent 17 mW/m^2;
    381 kg of sample.
  A programme able to do all that robotically in 1969-1973 could have
  landed a man.  This is a COMPARATIVE ENGINEERING JUDGEMENT, not a proof:
  it says the fake requires more capability than the landing, which the
  hypothesis exists to deny.

